 !!! Note
     The minimum last stake amount can be configured using `last_stake_amount_min_ratio` - which defaults to 0.5 (50%). This means that the minimum stake amount that's ever used is `stake_amount * 0.5`. This avoids very low stake amounts, that are close to the minimum tradable amount for the pair and can be refused by the exchange.
 
+#### Limit exposure to a single underlying currency
+
+If you trade several pairs that are all priced against, or margined in, the same underlying currency (e.g. several BTC-margined futures, or several coins quoted in the same stablecoin), a single adverse move in that underlying currency can hit all of those positions at once, even though each trade looked fine in isolation.
+
+To limit this concentration risk, set `max_underlying_currency_exposure` to the maximum fraction of your [tradable capital](#tradable-balance) that may be committed to open trades sharing the same quote/settlement currency at the same time.
+
+For example, with `max_underlying_currency_exposure=0.3`, and 3 open (or being opened) trades priced in USDT that together already tie up 25% of the tradable capital, a new USDT-quoted trade will be sized down to fit within the remaining 5%. If the available headroom is below the pair's minimum tradable amount, the trade is not opened.
+
+This limit is applied on top of all other stake-sizing options (`stake_amount`, `tradable_balance_ratio`, `available_capital`, per-pair exchange limits, `custom_stake_amount`, ...), and is evaluated identically in live/dry-run and in backtesting, so backtest results reflect the same sizing behavior the bot will use live.
+
+!!! Note
+    The limit only affects pairs sharing the same quote currency (spot) or settlement currency (futures). It has no effect if all your open trades already use the same `stake_currency`, which is the default pairlist behavior.
+
 #### Static stake amount
 
 The `stake_amount` configuration statically configures the amount of stake-currency your bot will use for each trade.
